## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to design deterministic end-to-end tests for complex user flows controlled by remote configuration. It demonstrates understanding of feature flag architecture, test data management, and handling asynchronous state changes. The question also probes awareness of telemetry validation and regression risks when configuration changes mid-session.

## Key Concepts

- **Feature Flags**: Remote configuration controlling feature availability without code deployment.
- **Test Data Seeding**: Creating reproducible user states and regional variants.
- **State Validation**: Verifying correct UI renders based on flag state.
- **Analytics Integration**: Ensuring telemetry events fire accurately per business requirements.
- **Regression Testing**: Confirming mid-session flag changes don't corrupt user state.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for NewUserTour feature flag
describe('NewUserTour Feature Flag', () => {
  let mockFlagService;

  beforeEach(() => {
    mockFlagService = new MockFeatureFlagService();
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', false);
  });

  test('OFF state: user bypasses onboarding', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('ON state: onboarding completes successfully', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-1')).toBeVisible();
    await page.click('[data-testid="next-step"]');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-2')).toBeVisible();
    await page.click('[data-testid="complete-onboarding"]');
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('mid-session toggle: onboarding aborts when flag turns OFF', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-1')).toBeVisible();
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', false);
    await page.reload();
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('regional variation: EU users see GDPR consent step', async ({ page }) => {
    seedUser({ region: 'EU' });
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.gdpr-consent-step')).toBeVisible();
  });

  test('analytics: events fire on step completion', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    const analytics = new MockAnalytics();
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await page.click('[data-testid="next-step"]');
    expect(analytics.events).toContainEqual(
      expect.objectContaining({ name: 'onboarding_step_completed', step: 1 })
    );
  });
});
```

## Evaluation Criteria

- Correctly mocks the remote flag service
- Covers both ON and OFF paths
- Tests at least one regional variation
- Validates UI state at each step
- Confirms analytics events fire appropriately
- Demonstrates handling of mid-session flag changes

## Why This Is Asked
Tests spanning multi-tenant boundaries and asynchronous export flows surface data isolation and resilience issues. This question probes end-to-end coverage for real-world SaaS data export use cases like Airbnb and Microsoft enterprise products.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data isolation across services
- Async background jobs and eventual consistency
- End-to-end validation of blob storage and email delivery
- Idempotent retries and fault injection
- Test data strategy and environment isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test outline (Playwright + API stubs)
test('tenant-scoped data export end-to-end', async ({ page, api, storage }) => {
  await loginAsAdmin(page);
  await page.click('#start-export');
  await page.waitForResponse(r => r.url.includes('/exports/').and(r.status() === 200));
  const status = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
  const blob = await storage.getBlob(`exports/${tenantId}/data.json`);
  expect(blob).toContain(`"tenantId":"${tenantId}"`);
  // idempotence
  await page.click('#start-export');
  const status2 = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status2).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you parameterize test data for tenants with different data shapes?
- How would you enforce data privacy in the test environment while validating exports?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests beginners learn to control external dependencies (maps, geolocation) and test isolation. This prompts demonstration of end-to-end coverage for critical user journeys while addressing flakiness through retries and data cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Test data isolation per run
- Geolocation and permission prompts
- Network throttling and flakiness handling
- Stubbing external APIs (maps)
- Session cleanup to prevent leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright example: mock geolocation and slow network
const context = await browser.newContext({
  geolocation: { latitude: 37.7749, longitude: -122.4194 }
});
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.overridePermissions('https://ride.example.com', ['geolocation']);
await page.route('**/maps/api/**', route => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ predictions: [] }) }));
```

